    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d772">

                <pb facs="0826" n="728" sameAs="frus:frus1932v04/pg728"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">123 C 353/199: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-01-04T14:00:00+08:00">January 4, 1932—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received January 4—8:10 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>11. Following from American Consul [General], Mukden: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“January 3, noon. Consul Chamberlain, on the way to the station <pb
                                facs="0827" n="729" xml:id="pg729"/> in my car at 6:30 a.m., was
                            stopped by three Japanese soldiers who, after he had identified himself
                            by card and passport, without justification attacked him, striking him
                            in the face many times. Face badly bruised but otherwise uninjured.
                            Incident verbally reported to Japanese Consulate General with the
                            request that severe penalty be meted out.</p>
                        <p>Chamberlain will leave for Harbin this afternoon.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>Legation has brought the foregoing orally to the attention of the Japanese Chargé
                    d’Affaires here and is requesting Myers to report result of his
                    representation.</p>
                <p>Repeated to the Department.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
